  // ── Content: Drafting Tip of the Day ──────────────────────────────
  var TIPS = [
    { t: "“Best efforts” vs “reasonable efforts”", d: "Some courts read 'best efforts' as more demanding; others see no difference. Don't leave it to the judge — define the standard, or pick one term and use it consistently." },
    { t: "Reserve “shall” for obligations", d: "'Shall' = a duty of a party. Use 'will' for statements of futurity, 'may' for discretion. Mixed usage is the most common self-inflicted ambiguity in contracts." },
    { t: "Don't define what you don't reuse", d: "A defined term used once is dead weight; a defined term never used signals sloppy assembly to the other side's reviewer. Define only what appears two or more times." },
    { t: "“Including” — settle it once", d: "Put 'including means including without limitation' in the interpretation section, applying globally — instead of sprinkling 'without limitation' through the document and implying the bare word is limiting everywhere else." },
    { t: "Make notices land", d: "A notice clause needs three things: permitted methods, the address or contact, and WHEN notice becomes effective (receipt vs dispatch). Most notice disputes hide in the third." },
    { t: "Kill “and/or”", d: "Write 'X or Y or both.' Courts have called 'and/or' a verbal monstrosity — and it litigates exactly as badly as it reads." },
    { t: "Survival needs a list", d: "'Termination shall not affect accrued rights' is not a survival clause. Name the sections that survive — confidentiality, indemnity, liability caps, governing law — and for how long." },
    { t: "Defend ≠ indemnify ≠ hold harmless", d: "'Defend' means paying the lawyers as the claim proceeds; 'indemnify' means reimbursing the loss; 'hold harmless' arguably waives your own claims. Choose each verb on purpose." },
    { t: "Business days or calendar days?", d: "'Within 30 days' invites a fight over weekends, holidays, and the start date. Specify calendar vs business days and the trigger: 'within 30 days after receipt of the invoice.'" },
    { t: "Cross-reference by number AND name", d: "'Section 8.2 (Indemnification)' — if a renumbering breaks the reference, the mismatched name flags it. A bare 'Section 8.2' pointing at the wrong section is a silent time bomb." },
    { t: "Indemnity inside or outside the cap?", d: "The most-litigated limitation-of-liability question: do indemnification obligations sit under the cap? Say so expressly. Either answer is fine; silence is not." },
    { t: "“Time is of the essence” is a loaded gun", d: "It can convert a one-day delay into grounds for termination. Deploy it only on the dates where that is truly the intent — not as boilerplate across the whole agreement." },
    { t: "Finish the consent phrase", d: "'Consent not to be unreasonably withheld' — add 'conditioned or delayed.' Slow-walking and onerous conditions are the real-world abuses, not flat refusal." },
    { t: "Change of control ≠ assignment (maybe)", d: "Courts split on whether a merger 'assigns' a contract. If you care who ends up across the table, address change of control expressly in the anti-assignment clause." },
    { t: "Don't hide obligations in recitals", d: "Whereas clauses are generally non-operative. If a duty matters, put it in the body — a recital-only obligation may simply not be enforceable." },
    { t: "Mind the here-words", d: "'Herein,' 'hereunder,' 'hereof' are ambiguous about scope: this section or this agreement? Write 'in this Agreement' or 'in this Section 4' and delete the here-word." },
